xnu 0221: a submission writes back only the buffers the CPU can have written

A frame submits ~95 buffers and the driver wrote back every one of them
(`mondrian-gl: frame 540: ... 34260 us (2963 handing on, 21497 waiting, 1
copying out)`, 29 fps). The display surface alone is 18 MiB of that, and the
writeback is a getPhysicalSegment and a flush_dcache64 -- `dc civac` a line at
a time, dmb and dsb either side -- per 4 KiB page: ~4500 of them, every submit,
and nothing in this task has written a pixel of that surface (expected; the log
line below settles it on a device).

The trap that makes this delicate is 0182's: the CP fetches VBOs, shader consts
and draw-state fragments with its own unprivileged access, so a buffer the CPU
filled but did not write back is read as zeros and the SQE halts on an unknown
opcode. So the question a submission must answer is what the CPU *can* have
written, not what it says it wrote: freedreno returns from fd_bo_cpu_prep
without an ioctl for a buffer it does not share, so MSM_GEM_CPU_PREP is not a
per-write signal and a flag set there would miss every VBO in the frame. A
buffer is written back when

	- the task mapped it (clientMemoryForType). That mapping is the only way
	  a write from this task reaches the buffer, and it may write at any
	  time afterwards, so this one is never cleared;
	- it is dirty: fresh memory (the zero fill sits in the cache) or written
	  by this driver (the gpu* rewrites); bufferSync clears it;
	- or it is one of this submission's command streams, which the CP fetches
	  itself.

Everything else -- the display surface, render targets the GPU only writes,
textures it only samples -- cannot have been written from this task at all, and
its one initial writeback covers its lifetime.

gpusyncall=1 restores the old behaviour exactly: one boot argument, for a boot
where the CP reads a buffer stale. The first four submissions log which policy
ran and what it moved, next to the frame line's `waiting`:
`wrote back 46 of 95 buffers (1024 KiB), held 49 back (22120 KiB), 812 us`.

bufferSync also steps by the segment the descriptor returns instead of by page.
It flushed `length` bytes and then advanced 0x1000, so a run of contiguous pages
was flushed once for every page in it, each flush paying its own dsb.
